{ "source": "// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity >=0.8.0;

contract DecompiledContract {
    uint256 public count;

    function Unresolved_2a1afcd9() public view returns (bytes memory) {
        return _readStorageBytes(1);
    }

    function Unresolved_4ed3885e(uint256 arg0) public {
        count = 0x01 + (arg0 + arg0);
    }

    function Unresolved_6d4ce63c() public view returns (bytes memory) {
        return _readStorageBytes(1);
    }

    function _readStorageBytes(uint256 slot) internal view returns (bytes memory) {
        uint256 length;
        uint256 data;
        assembly {
            let packed := sload(slot)
            switch and(packed, 0x1)
            case 0 {
                length := sload(add(slot, 0x1))
                data := sload(add(slot, 0x2))
            }
            default {
                length := shr(0x1, packed)
                data := shr(0x8, packed)
            }
        }
        if ((0x1f & length) == 0) {
            return abi.encodePacked(0x20, length);
        } else {
            uint256 mask = ~(0x0100 ** (0x20 - (0x1f & length)) - 0x01);
            return abi.encodePacked(0x20, length, mask & data);
        }
    }
}" ,"notes": "The assembly `if ... else` construct is invalid in Yul (if has no else branch); replaced with `switch/case/default`. Also replaced `!(0x1f & length)` with a boolean comparison, since `!` requires a bool operand in Solidity, and moved the mask computation into a typed variable."}

message.reasoning_content:
The issue: `if and(packed, 0x1) { ... } else { ... }` — assembly if doesn't support else. Convert to if/else statement properly: use `switch` or restructure. Also `0x0100 ** ...` in assembly? No, that's outside assembly — fine but `~(...)` on uint256 fine. Actually the else inside assembly is the error. Fix: use switch or two ifs.

Also `data` unused warning fine. Rewrite assembly:

```
assembly {
    let packed := sload(slot)
    switch and(packed, 0x1)
    case 0 {
        length := sload(add(slot, 0x1))
        data := sload(add(slot, 0x2))
    }
    default {
        length := shr(0x1, packed)
        data := shr(0x8, packed)
    }
}
```

Also `0x0100 ** (0x20 - (0x1f & length))` — exponent uint256 ok; `~x` where x is uint256... `0x0100 ** (...) - 0x01` fine. But `~` literal expression: `~(...)` needs uint type; the expression is uint256, ok. But `0x20 - (0x1f & length)`: 0x1f & length is uint256, ok.

But wait: `if (!(0x1f & length))` — `!` on uint256 not allowed in Solidity? Actually `!` requires bool. `!(0x1f & length)` invalid. Change to `if (0x1f & length == 0)`. Hmm but the error was only at else. Still fix for correctness. Also `abi.encodePacked(0x20, length)` — mixing uint8 literal and uint256 fine.

Also `~(...) & data` — data declared but assigned only in assembly; fine.

Provide fixed contract.
